<chapter xml:id="ch-episode-model">
  <title>The Episode Model</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The theoretical framework in <xref ref="ch-theoretical-foundation" text="title"/>
      explains why the project attends to mathematical control while remaining cautious
      about claims concerning student knowledge. The <term>Episode Model</term> provides
      the corresponding response-level analytic unit. It represents the underlying
      links visible in a short written response without treating that response
      as a complete conception or a transcript of the student's thought process.
    </p>

    <p>
      The model separates three questions that are easily blurred in ordinary coding:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>What mathematical condition or feature does the response cite?</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>How does the response connect that condition to a conclusion?</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>What conclusion does the response draw?</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      This separation allows researchers to distinguish a false observation from an
      invalid inference, an incomplete link from a complete one, and a sound
      local argument from an answer that addresses every part of the question.
    </p>

    <p>
      This chapter is the canonical source for the model's terminology, notation, and
      current policy choices. It defines the analytic objects. It does not prescribe the
      step-by-step coding workflow, the spreadsheet schema, or the computational
      implementation. Those belong to <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/> and
      the pipeline chapters.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-episode-definition">
    <title>Definition and Notation</title>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-episode-sequence">
      <title>Episode Sequences</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>x</m> denote one student's written response to a particular question. The
        response may contain no identifiable justification, one local justification, or
        several related justifications. The ordered collection extracted from the
        response is the <term>episode sequence</term>
        <md>
          E(x)=\langle e_1,e_2,\ldots,e_n\rangle.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The order follows the development of the written reasoning, normally by the
        order in which the judgments appear. The sequence is therefore not merely an
        unordered set of codes. It preserves whether one conclusion becomes the basis
        for another and whether several reasons are offered separately.
      </p>

      <p>
        A response with no identifiable criterion-to-judgment structure has no complete
        episode. Depending on the governing screening policy, the response may be
        excluded, preserved as partial evidence, or routed for review. The existence of
        a mathematically correct final answer does not, by itself, create an episode.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <assemblage xml:id="subsec-episode-components">
      <title>Criterion, Inferential Link, and Judgment</title>

      <p>
        Each episode has the form
        <md>
          e_k=(C_k,I_k,J_k),
        </md>
        where the components have distinct roles.
        <dl width="medium">
          <li>
            <title>Criterion, <m>C_k</m></title>
            <p>
              The observation, fact, condition, intermediate result, or mathematical
              feature cited as a reason. It is what the response points to. Examples
              include the existence of a free variable, the number of pivots, the sign of
              a derivative, or the absence of a contradictory row.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Inferential link, <m>I_k</m></title>
            <p>
              The justification link that carries the criterion to the judgment. It states 
              why the cited feature supports the conclusion in the context of the question.
              The link may be stated directly, recoverable from the wording, invalid, or not
              recoverable at all.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Judgment, <m>J_k</m></title>
            <p>
              The conclusion, classification, validation, or decision drawn from the
              criterion. It is what the response settles, such as concluding that a
              system is consistent, that a solution is not unique, or that a function is
              increasing.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      In compact form,
      <md>
        \text{criterion}
        \quad\underset{\text{link}}{\xrightarrow{\text{inferential}}}\quad
        \text{judgment}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-episode-canonical">
      <title>One Complete Episode</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Consider the response:</p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>There is a free variable, so the solution cannot be unique.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          One defensible representation is
          <md>
            e_1=(C_1,I_1,J_1),
          </md>
          where
          <dl width="narrow">
            <li>
              <title><m>C_1</m></title>
              <p>A free variable exists.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title><m>I_1</m></title>
              <p>
                In the stated context, the existence of a free variable rules out one
                unique solution.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <title><m>J_1</m></title>
              <p>The solution is not unique.</p>
            </li>
          </dl>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
      The criterion and judgment should remain as close as possible to the claims the
      student actually made. The inferential link may require a concise analytic
      paraphrase, but that paraphrase must represent the connection supported by the
      response rather than a stronger argument supplied by the researcher.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-episode-visible-trace">
      <title>A Visible Trace, Not a Mental State</title>

      <p>
        An episode is a <term>visible trace of justification</term>. It records evidence
        present in one response to one question. It does not claim to describe everything
        the student knew, believed, considered, or did before writing.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Episode Model is motivated by the cK¢ framework, especially its treatment of
        control structures and validation, but the triple <m>(C,I,J)</m> is a
        project-developed analytic device. It is not part of the original conception
        quadruplet <m>(P,R,L,\Sigma)</m>. An episode may provide evidence related to a
        control structure; it is not itself a complete conception.
      </p>

      <p>
        Appropriate claims therefore remain tied to the response:
        <ul>
          <li>the response cites a particular criterion;</li>
          <li>the written link contains a particular episode; or</li>
          <li>the response provides evidence related to a control structure.</li>
        </ul>
        Claims about stable beliefs, complete conceptions, or reasoning not visible in
        the response require additional evidence beyond the Episode Model.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-episode-evidence-status">
    <title>Evidence Status and Completeness</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="subsec-episode-explicit-implicit-absent">
      <title>Explicit, Implicit, and Absent Links</title>

      <p>
        The criterion and judgment usually correspond to claims made in the response.
        The inferential link is often less direct. The project records its evidence
        status using three values.
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title><c>explicit</c></title>
            <p>
              The response directly states the relevant relationship or justification. Words
              such as <q>because</q>, <q>since</q>, <q>so</q>, and <q>therefore</q> may
              signal an explicit connection, although a connector word alone does not
              guarantee that the link is mathematically valid.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title><c>implicit</c></title>
            <p>
              The link is not stated directly, but the connection is recoverable from
              the response without adding a new mathematical idea or repairing the
              argument. A careful reader can state the minimal link needed to express
              the relation already supported by the writing.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title><c>absent</c></title>
            <p>
              The response contains a criterion and a judgment, but no link connecting
              them can be recovered without guessing, importing an unsupported idea, or
              replacing the student's reasoning with a better argument.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        These values describe the <em>link only</em>. Under the current protocol, an
        absent link does not by itself make the episode incomplete. If both a criterion
        and a judgment are identifiable, the episode remains complete, with the link
        recorded as absent. Its inferential-validity digit is then <m>0</m>, because no
        valid link supports the stated step.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <assemblage xml:id="subsec-episode-complete-incomplete">
      <title>Complete and Incomplete Episodes</title>

      <p>
        Under the current policy, an episode is <term>complete</term> when both its
        criterion <m>C_k</m> and judgment <m>J_k</m> are identifiable. Its inferential
        link <m>I_k</m> is then classified as <c>explicit</c>, <c>implicit</c>, or 
        <c>absent</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        An episode is <term>incomplete</term> when either the criterion or the judgment is
        missing or too ambiguous to identify responsibly. For example,
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p><q>There is a free variable.</q></p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        supplies a possible criterion but no judgment. The student might intend to rule
        out a unique solution, assert infinitely many solutions, identify a variable, or
        make some other claim. Choosing among those conclusions would add information
        not present in the response.
      </p>

      <p>
        Similarly, a bare answer such as <q>No, the solution is not unique</q> provides a
        judgment but no criterion. It may answer the question correctly, but it does not
        identify the link used to reach that answer.
      </p>

      <p>
        Incomplete episodes may be preserved as partial evidence, but they do not receive
        the two-digit correctness annotation defined in
        <xref ref="subsec-episode-two-digit-code" text="title"/>. Blank correctness
        fields mean <q>not eligible for scoring</q>; they must not be replaced by
        <m>(0,0)</m>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-episode-no-repair">
      <title>Do Not Repair the Response</title>

      <p>
        The analytic task is to represent the link the student provided, not
        the stronger link the student could have provided. A researcher may
        clarify wording, normalize synonymous mathematical expressions, and record the
        minimal link sufficient to license the step. A researcher may not supply a missing criterion,
        invent a judgment, or import a mathematical step merely because it would make
        the argument correct.
      </p>

      <p>
        The boundary is between <term>recovery</term> and <term>invention</term>.
        Recovery makes an already supported relation explicit. Invention adds a relation
        that the response does not support. When that boundary is uncertain, the
        uncertainty should be recorded and reviewed rather than concealed through a
        polished reconstruction.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-episode-relations">
    <title>Relations Among Episodes</title>

    <p>
      A response may contain several episodes. Their relationship is part of the
      analytic structure and should be represented separately from the codes assigned to
      the individual episodes.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-episode-chained">
      <title>Chained Episodes</title>

      <p>
        Episodes are <term>chained</term> when the judgment of one episode becomes the
        criterion of another. Formally, for distinct episodes <m>e_i</m> and <m>e_j</m>,
        a chain occurs when
        <md>
          J_i=C_j
        </md>
        up to an acceptable mathematical paraphrase.
      </p>

      <p>
        For example,
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          There are fewer pivots than variables, so there is a free variable.
          Therefore, the solution cannot be unique.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        contains the chain
        <md>
          \begin{aligned}
            e_1 \amp:
            \text{fewer pivots than variables}
            \longrightarrow
            \text{a free variable exists},\\
            e_2 \amp:
            \text{a free variable exists}
            \longrightarrow
            \text{the solution is not unique}.
          \end{aligned}
        </md>
        The first judgment supplies the next criterion.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-episode-independent">
      <title>Independent Episodes</title>

      <p>
        Episodes are <term>independent</term> when they provide separate,
        non-interacting justifications. Neither episode uses the other episode's
        judgment as a criterion or sub-step. Independent episodes may support the same
        conclusion through different mathematical ideas, such as a pivot argument and a
        dimension argument, or they may address different parts of a multipart prompt.
      </p>

      <p>
        Independence is a relation among reasoning moves, not a claim that the ideas are
        unrelated within mathematics. It means only that the written response presents
        them as separate lines of justification.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-episode-nested">
      <title>Nested Episodes</title>

      <p>
        Episodes are <term>nested</term> when one identifiable episode functions as a
        sub-step inside a larger criterion-to-judgment argument. A nested episode may
        also be chained with another episode; the two terms describe different features
        and are not mutually exclusive.
      </p>

      <p>
        The existence of nested structure is an analytic observation. The decision to
        retain the parent episode, the child episode, or both is a coding policy. The
        mathematics alone does not determine that choice.
      </p>

      <remark xml:id="rem-episode-nesting-policy">
        <title>Current Nesting Policy: Open</title>
        <p>
          The source materials do not yet contain a ratified rule for collapsing nested
          episodes. Until that policy is fixed, the working rule is to preserve every
          defensible episode, record the relation among them, and avoid silently merging
          or deleting a level. A final policy must be frozen before formal validation, as
          described in <xref ref="ch-study-design" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </remark>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-episode-correctness">
    <title>Correctness Overlay</title>

    <p>
      The Episode Model describes the structure of a justification. Correctness is a
      separate annotation placed on complete episodes. This separation prevents the
      model from treating a mathematically wrong episode as though it were not an
      episode and prevents a correct final answer from being mistaken for evidence of a
      particular justification.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-episode-normative-reference">
      <title>Normative Mathematical Reference</title>

      <p>
        Correctness is evaluated against a <term>normative reference</term> prepared for
        the question. It contains two kinds of information:
        <ul>
          <li>
            the actual mathematical state of the problem, used to determine whether a
            stated criterion is true; and
          </li>
          <li>
            the question-specific valid-inference set <m>\mathcal{T}_q</m>, containing
            the criterion-to-judgment links accepted as mathematically valid for question
            <m>q</m>.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The reference must include all conditions supplied by the question. For example,
        the existence of a free variable rules out a unique solution when the system is
        known to be consistent. Without consistency, the same observation does not by
        itself establish that solutions exist. Link validity is therefore
        question-relative rather than a universal relation between isolated phrases.
      </p>

      <p>
        The normative reference is developed and versioned with the question-specific
        analytic frame described in
        <xref ref="ch-codebook" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-episode-two-digit-code">
      <title>Criterion and Link Correctness</title>

      <p>
        Each complete episode receives the correctness annotation
        <md>
          \operatorname{code}(e_k)=(c_k^1,c_k^2)\in\{0,1\}^2,
        </md>
        where the two digits answer different questions.
      </p>

      <dl width="narrow">
        <li>
          <title>Criterion correctness, <m>c_k^1</m></title>
          <p>
            Set <m>c_k^1=1</m> when the criterion <m>C_k</m> is true in the actual
            mathematical state and <m>c_k^1=0</m> when it is false.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Inferential validity, <m>c_k^2</m></title>
          <p>
            Set <m>c_k^2=1</m> when the link <m>I_k</m> validly supports the judgment
            <m>J_k</m> in the question context and <m>c_k^2=0</m> when the link is
            invalid or absent.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>

      <p>
        The possible annotations are:
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-episode-correctness-codes">
        <title>Interpretation of the two-digit correctness annotation.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="18%"/>
          <col width="82%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Code</cell>
            <cell>Interpretation</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>(1,1)</m></cell>
            <cell>True criterion and valid inferential link.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>(1,0)</m></cell>
            <cell>True criterion, but the inferential link is invalid or absent.</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>(0,1)</m></cell>
            <cell>
              False criterion, although the general criterion-to-judgment relationship
              is valid.
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>(0,0)</m></cell>
            <cell>False criterion and invalid or absent inferential link.</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        The digits are independent. A student may cite a true feature but use it through
        an invalid inference, producing <m>(1,0)</m>. Conversely, the general inference
        may be valid even though the criterion is false in the student's computed state,
        producing <m>(0,1)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Incomplete episodes receive no correctness annotation because one of the claims
        required for evaluation is missing. A blank annotation means <q>not scored</q>,
        not <q>both parts are wrong.</q>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-episode-local-global">
      <title>Local Link and Goal Coverage</title>

      <p>
        The correctness annotation evaluates one local reasoning move. It does not
        determine whether the response fully answers the question. The project therefore
        keeps <term>link quality</term> and <term>goal coverage</term> separate.
      </p>

      <p>
        Goal coverage records which required part or parts of the question are addressed
        by an episode's judgment. A judgment may cover one listed goal, several goals,
        none of the goals, or have unclear coverage. For example, the response
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>There are two pivots, so one variable is free.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        may contain a <m>(1,1)</m> episode while failing to identify the free variable,
        name the basic variables, or state whether the solution is unique. The
        link is sound, but the response has only partial goal coverage.
      </p>

      <p>
        The converse can also occur: a response may state the requested final answer but
        support it with an invalid inference or no codable justification. Thus:
        <ul>
          <li>a sound episode does not guarantee a complete answer;</li>
          <li>a correct answer does not guarantee a sound episode; and</li>
          <li>a bare correct answer does not identify a control structure.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The current source materials specify goal coverage for individual episode
        judgments. They do not yet supply a final formal aggregation rule for combining
        all episode-level coverage annotations into a single response-level score. That
        rule should be fixed with the coding protocol before confirmatory validation.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-episode-summary">
    <title>Notation and Policy Summary</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-episode-policy-summary">
      <title>Canonical Episode Model notation and current policy.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="24%"/>
        <col width="32%"/>
        <col width="44%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Item</cell>
          <cell>Notation or values</cell>
          <cell>Canonical meaning or current policy</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Response</cell>
          <cell><m>x</m></cell>
          <cell>One written answer interpreted in its exact question context.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Episode sequence</cell>
          <cell><m>E(x)=\langle e_1,\ldots,e_n\rangle</m></cell>
          <cell>Ordered local justifications extracted from the response.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Episode</cell>
          <cell><m>e_k=(C_k,I_k,J_k)</m></cell>
          <cell>Criterion, inferential link, and judgment.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Link status</cell>
          <cell><c>explicit</c>, <c>implicit</c>, <c>absent</c></cell>
          <cell>
            Describes the evidence for the link. An absent link does not alone make the
            episode incomplete.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Completeness</cell>
          <cell><c>complete</c>, <c>incomplete</c></cell>
          <cell>
            Complete when both criterion and judgment are identifiable; incomplete when
            either is missing or irreducibly ambiguous.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Episode relations</cell>
          <cell><c>chained(k)</c>, <c>independent</c>, blank</cell>
          <cell>
            Preserve identifiable structure. Blank records a lone episode. Detected
            nesting is an analytic observation preserved in <c>notes</c>, not a sheet
            value; the final nested-episode retention policy remains open.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Correctness</cell>
          <cell><m>(c_k^1,c_k^2)</m></cell>
          <cell>
            Criterion truth and inferential validity, assigned only to complete episodes.
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Goal coverage</cell>
          <cell>question-goal labels</cell>
          <cell>
            Records which requested outcomes an episode's judgment addresses; kept
            separate from episode correctness.
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      The Episode Model represents a response as an ordered sequence of local
      criterion-to-judgment moves. It preserves the inferential link, distinguishes
      complete from partial evidence, records relations among multiple episodes, and
      overlays criterion truth and inferential validity without confusing either with
      full-question completion. The next chapter connects these response-level episodes
      to the project's shared control-structure library and question-specific normative
      information.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</chapter>